{"id":"minimum-window-substring","title":"Minimum Window Substring","difficulty":"Hard","pattern":"Sliding Window","tags":["Hash Table","String","Sliding Window"],"companies":{"google":3,"apple":2,"meta":4,"amazon":3,"microsoft":3},"description":"Given strings `s` and `t`, return the shortest substring of `s` that contains every character of `t` **with multiplicity** (if `t` has two `a`s, the window needs at least two `a`s). Return `\"\"` if no such window exists. The answer is guaranteed to be unique when it exists.","examples":[{"input":"s = \"ADOBECODEBANC\", t = \"ABC\"","output":"\"BANC\"","explanation":"`BANC` is the shortest stretch that has an A, a B and a C."},{"input":"s = \"a\", t = \"a\"","output":"\"a\""},{"input":"s = \"a\", t = \"aa\"","output":"\"\"","explanation":"Two `a`s are required but `s` has only one."}],"constraints":["1 <= s.length, t.length <= 10^5","s and t consist of uppercase and lowercase English letters."],"hints":["Grow a window to the right until it covers all of t, then shrink from the left while it still covers t; record the best, then keep going.","Keep a count map of what t needs and a counter `formed` of how many distinct characters currently meet their required count.","The window is complete when `formed` equals the number of distinct characters in t. Only update `formed` when a count crosses the required threshold in either direction."],"function":"minWindow","signature":"def minWindow(self, s: str, t: str) -> str:","arg_types":{"s":"str","t":"str"},"return_type":"str","compare":"exact","tests":[{"input":{"s":"ADOBECODEBANC","t":"ABC"},"output":"BANC"},{"input":{"s":"a","t":"a"},"output":"a"},{"input":{"s":"a","t":"aa"},"output":""},{"input":{"s":"ab","t":"b"},"output":"b"},{"input":{"s":"aa","t":"aa"},"output":"aa"},{"input":{"s":"xyz","t":"q"},"output":""},{"input":{"s":"abcabdebac","t":"cda"},"output":"cabd"},{"input":{"s":"aAbB","t":"Ab"},"output":"Ab"},{"input":{"s":"","t":"abc"},"output":"abc"},{"input":{"s":"","t":"aaa"},"output":"aaa"}],"solution":"class Solution:\n    def minWindow(self, s: str, t: str) -> str:\n        if len(t) > len(s):\n            return \"\"\n        need = Counter(t)\n        required = len(need)       # distinct characters that must be satisfied\n        window = defaultdict(int)\n        formed = 0                 # distinct characters currently satisfied\n        best_len = float(\"inf\")\n        best_start = 0\n        left = 0\n        for right, ch in enumerate(s):\n            window[ch] += 1\n            if ch in need and window[ch] == need[ch]:\n                formed += 1\n            while formed == required:\n                if right - left + 1 < best_len:\n                    best_len = right - left + 1\n                    best_start = left\n                out = s[left]\n                window[out] -= 1\n                if out in need and window[out] < need[out]:\n                    formed -= 1\n                left += 1\n        return \"\" if best_len == float(\"inf\") else s[best_start:best_start + best_len]\n","approach":"**Expand to satisfy, contract to minimise.** Keep a map of what `t` needs and a map of what the window holds. `formed` counts how many distinct characters of `t` are fully satisfied. Move `right` to the right until `formed == required`; then move `left` to the right as far as possible while the window stays complete, recording the shortest complete window seen. Continue until `right` reaches the end.\n\n**Why it works:** for every position of `left`, the first `right` that completes the window gives the minimal window starting at `left`; the inner `while` enumerates those `left` values in increasing order and never needs to move `left` back. Each index enters and leaves the window once, so the total work is linear.\n\n**Common mistakes:** incrementing `formed` every time a needed character arrives rather than only when its count *reaches* the requirement (overcounts duplicates); shrinking with `if` instead of `while`; building the substring at every step instead of storing `(start, length)`; forgetting the empty-result case.\n\n**Interview follow-ups:** Characters must appear in order as a subsequence (Minimum Window Subsequence, a different two-pointer scan). Return all minimum windows if uniqueness is not promised. Streaming `s` with limited memory (keep only the window and counts, which this already does).","complexity":{"time":"O(|s| + |t|)","space":"O(alphabet)"},"similar":["permutation-in-string","longest-substring-without-repeating-characters","sliding-window-maximum"],"number":29,"starter":"class Solution:\n    def minWindow(self, s: str, t: str) -> str:\n        pass\n"}